<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wessex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 519–927</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Kingdom of Wessex, Kingdom of the West Saxons</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wessex was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdom of the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>. From the 9th century it absorbed Kent, Sussex, Surrey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> it alone survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> conquests. His descendants extended their rule over the other English kingdoms, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Athelstan</a> became king of all England in 927.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wessex.jpg" alt="Wessex" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philg88; Attribution: Wikimedia Foundation (www.wikimedia.org) · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_British_Isles_9th_century.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
